00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.920
No existing projects were referenced in the thread.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.880
Pop the hood on expert prefetching in mixture of experts' inference on consumer hardware.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.460
MoE models keep most parameters off the GPU until needed, yet loading experts from SSD during

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.600
generation creates a hard latency wall.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.640
The key mechanism is a small pre-fetch buffer that predicts the next layer's active experts

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.140
from the current Tokens router low jits and issues asynchronous reads while the current

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.000
layer computes.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.860
This adds roughly 200 milliseconds of pipeline latency per request, but cuts peak VRAM

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.160
usage by more than half compared with keeping every expert resident.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:56.460
On a 68GB model running on a 16GB card, the pre-fetch version sustains 20 tokens per second

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.220
once the working set warms, versus one to two tokens per second with plain M-map.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.020
The quality tradeoff is zero because the pre-fetch is bid exact.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.120
The only cost is the extra SSD bandwidth during cold starts.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.740
Teams should enable pre-fetch when VRAM is under 30% of model size

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.080
and disable it when the entire expert set already fits,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.520
because the added latency then has no offsetting memory win.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:25.880
Try H2O Lightning 4B for insurance claim triage or multi-browser agents

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.900
where you need calibrated yes-no or choice scores in one forward pass.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:39.060
Run the QEN 3.635 BA3BQ4KM build on a 6GB card with the publish llama.cpp flags

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.420
if you need vision plus long context on modest hardware.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.920
Check the merged VLLM slash V1 slash system 1 support

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:52.660
if you serve type decision models and want native open AI compatible endpoints without extra shims.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.780
Explore Luma Browser now that it is open source if you want a single local environment that

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.420
handles model swapping, image generation, and scheduled agents.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:08.080
h2o.ai plans to release 12b and 31b versions of its decision model later this quarter.

